1
00:00:00,540 --> 00:00:02,620
17 miles.

2
00:00:02,690 --> 00:00:07,690
That's how far the Edmund Fitzgerald was from safety when she disappeared.

3
00:00:07,760 --> 00:00:12,980
17 miles from Whitefish Point, and the shelter of the bay behind it.

4
00:00:13,050 --> 00:00:16,150
At full speed, just over an hour.

5
00:00:16,220 --> 00:00:20,580
At 7:10, her captain radioed the ship behind him.

6
00:00:20,650 --> 00:00:23,710
"We are holding our own."

7
00:00:23,780 --> 00:00:25,920
Minutes later, she was gone.

8
00:00:25,990 --> 00:00:28,370
29 men.

9
00:00:28,440 --> 00:00:32,200
She went down at the edge of the most dangerous water on Lake Superior.

10
00:00:32,270 --> 00:00:36,020
Sailors call it the Graveyard of the Great Lakes.

11
00:00:36,090 --> 00:00:38,890
It has taken at least 240 ships.

12
00:00:38,960 --> 00:00:45,240
A steamer carrying 70 tons of silver ore that has never been accounted for.

13
00:00:45,310 --> 00:00:49,260
A freighter that exploded when the cold water reached her boilers.

14
00:00:49,330 --> 00:00:54,950
A captain who survived 20 hours clinging to the roof of his pilothouse.

15
00:00:55,020 --> 00:00:57,720
And almost all of them are still down there.

16
00:00:57,790 --> 00:00:59,840
So let's drain it.

17
00:00:59,910 --> 00:01:01,690
We can't really, of course.

18
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:07,750
But divers, sonar and 200 years of shipwreck records have shown us what's down there.

19
00:01:07,820 --> 00:01:11,456
So we're going to lower the water, a few feet at a time,

20
00:01:11,476 --> 00:01:13,480
and watch the graveyard appear.

21
00:01:13,550 --> 00:01:15,130
And watch for this.

22
00:01:15,200 --> 00:01:18,360
Every ship down there was killed by something.

23
00:01:18,430 --> 00:01:20,711
By the time we reach the bottom, 530 feet down,

24
00:01:20,731 --> 00:01:23,109
you'll see that on the night the Fitzgerald sank,

25
00:01:23,129 --> 00:01:25,410
every one of those killers was waiting for her.

26
00:01:29,080 --> 00:01:31,200
This is Great Lakes Files.

27
00:01:31,270 --> 00:01:35,480
We go looking for the places hidden in and under these lakes.

28
00:01:35,550 --> 00:01:40,470
A lot of you found us through our last Edmund Fitzgerald video, so welcome.

29
00:01:40,540 --> 00:01:43,160
Most people watching haven't subscribed yet.

30
00:01:43,230 --> 00:01:47,060
If you like this kind of story, tap subscribe right now.

31
00:01:47,130 --> 00:01:52,574
It's free, it takes one second, and it's what keeps this channel going.

32
00:01:52,644 --> 00:01:57,924
The point is called Whitefish Point, at the eastern end of Lake Superior.

33
00:01:57,994 --> 00:02:00,912
Every ship entering or leaving the lake has to pass it,

34
00:02:00,932 --> 00:02:04,974
and more ships have been lost around it than anywhere else on Lake Superior.

35
00:02:06,124 --> 00:02:08,614
Three things kill them here.

36
00:02:08,684 --> 00:02:09,684
The storm.

37
00:02:09,754 --> 00:02:12,426
Behind the point lie 160 miles of open water,

38
00:02:12,446 --> 00:02:16,734
so a gale from the northwest has room to build waves of 30 feet or more.

39
00:02:18,864 --> 00:02:19,634
The dark.

40
00:02:19,704 --> 00:02:24,323
Night, fog, snow squalls, even the smoke of forest fires,

41
00:02:24,343 --> 00:02:26,684
in a lane crowded with ships.

42
00:02:27,254 --> 00:02:28,404
And the cold.

43
00:02:28,474 --> 00:02:34,964
Lake Superior is so cold that, as sailors say, she seldom gives up her dead.

44
00:02:35,034 --> 00:02:36,534
Remember those three.

45
00:02:36,604 --> 00:02:39,244
The storm, the dark, and the cold.

46
00:02:39,314 --> 00:02:42,458
Let's start the drain.

47
00:02:42,528 --> 00:02:43,718
20 feet.

48
00:02:43,788 --> 00:02:47,346
Just west of the lighthouse, which has stood on the point since 1849,

49
00:02:47,366 --> 00:02:50,768
the bottom starts to show, and the first wreck appears, in pieces.

50
00:02:54,788 --> 00:02:56,738
The schooner barge Saturn.

51
00:02:56,808 --> 00:02:58,082
In November 1872,

52
00:02:58,102 --> 00:03:03,788
an arctic gale snapped the towline between her and the steamer pulling her.

53
00:03:04,608 --> 00:03:08,018
She was driven onto the bottom here and pounded apart.

54
00:03:08,088 --> 00:03:09,568
7 men died.

55
00:03:09,638 --> 00:03:11,188
The storm.

56
00:03:11,258 --> 00:03:12,298
30 feet.

57
00:03:12,368 --> 00:03:16,578
To the west, the wreck of the schooner Allegheny comes out of the water.

58
00:03:16,648 --> 00:03:18,984
She was stranded in a gale in 1913,

59
00:03:19,004 --> 00:03:23,628
and the lifesaving crew from Vermilion Point got every man off alive.

60
00:03:24,578 --> 00:03:28,040
Not every crew here was that lucky.

61
00:03:28,110 --> 00:03:33,860
At 50 feet, 1.5 miles from the point, a wooden steamer appears.

62
00:03:33,930 --> 00:03:36,300
Her name is the Myron.

63
00:03:36,370 --> 00:03:40,944
On November 22, 1919, she left Munising piled high with lumber,

64
00:03:40,964 --> 00:03:43,350
towing a barge called the Miztec.

65
00:03:45,540 --> 00:03:49,873
Two hours later, a November gale hit them, with winds of 60 miles an hour,

66
00:03:49,893 --> 00:03:52,520
heavy snow, and the temperature falling fast.

67
00:03:53,890 --> 00:03:57,940
The seas opened the seams of her old wooden hull.

68
00:03:58,010 --> 00:04:01,210
Ice built up on her until she could barely make headway.

69
00:04:01,280 --> 00:04:04,974
Her captain left the barge at anchor to ride out the storm,

70
00:04:04,994 --> 00:04:08,120
and fought on toward the shelter of Whitefish Bay.

71
00:04:08,190 --> 00:04:12,170
A bigger steel steamer ran alongside to shield her from the waves.

72
00:04:12,240 --> 00:04:15,660
A lifesaving crew followed in a small boat.

73
00:04:15,730 --> 00:04:17,470
1.5 miles from the point…

74
00:04:17,540 --> 00:04:20,140
the water reached her boiler fires.

75
00:04:20,210 --> 00:04:22,810
She sank in 4 minutes.

76
00:04:22,880 --> 00:04:27,860
Her crew got into the lifeboats, but they were trapped in a sea of floating lumber.

77
00:04:27,930 --> 00:04:30,440
Two steamers tried to reach them.

78
00:04:30,510 --> 00:04:35,690
One got close enough to throw them lines, but their hands were too frozen to hold on.

79
00:04:35,760 --> 00:04:41,030
Her captain was still inside the pilothouse when it tore away from the ship.

80
00:04:41,100 --> 00:04:44,810
He climbed out through the window and held on to the roof.

81
00:04:44,880 --> 00:04:49,082
20 hours later, a steamer coming up from the Soo found him, still on that roof,

82
00:04:49,102 --> 00:04:51,860
drifting miles away, his clothes frozen to his body.

83
00:04:55,160 --> 00:04:58,110
He was the only survivor.

84
00:04:58,180 --> 00:05:01,930
Remember what sailors say about Lake Superior and her dead?

85
00:05:02,000 --> 00:05:04,700
This time, the lake gave up every one.

86
00:05:04,770 --> 00:05:09,500
All 17 men were found, still in their life jackets, covered in ice.

87
00:05:09,570 --> 00:05:12,350
Some were found days later, in a lifeboat.

88
00:05:12,420 --> 00:05:18,090
Eight weren't found until the next spring, frozen into the ice along the shore.

89
00:05:18,160 --> 00:05:25,140
People from the towns around the bay cut them free and buried them in a small cemetery called Mission Hill.

90
00:05:25,700 --> 00:05:29,220
And the barge the Myron left at anchor, the Miztec?

91
00:05:29,290 --> 00:05:31,110
She rode out that storm.

92
00:05:31,180 --> 00:05:36,350
Two years later, she sank off the same shore with all 7 of her crew.

93
00:05:36,420 --> 00:05:41,252
Today she lies a few miles from the Myron, at the same depth.

94
00:05:41,322 --> 00:05:42,772
100 feet.

95
00:05:42,842 --> 00:05:47,992
7 miles out from the lighthouse, the schooner barge Niagara appears.

96
00:05:48,062 --> 00:05:54,502
In September 1887, she broke loose from the steamer towing her and went down.

97
00:05:54,572 --> 00:05:56,702
All 9 people on board were lost.

98
00:05:56,772 --> 00:06:01,022
At 120 feet, there's a steamer called the Vienna.

99
00:06:01,092 --> 00:06:07,012
She sank in 1873 after a collision, and everyone aboard got off alive.

100
00:06:07,082 --> 00:06:11,032
In this graveyard, that makes her one of the lucky ones.

101
00:06:11,102 --> 00:06:15,712
The water here is cold and clear, and wrecks last a long time in it.

102
00:06:15,782 --> 00:06:21,062
This is one of the last places in the Great Lakes where the wrecks aren't covered in zebra mussels.

103
00:06:21,132 --> 00:06:25,069
376 square miles of it are now a protected preserve,

104
00:06:25,089 --> 00:06:28,112
and divers call it an underwater museum.

105
00:06:29,382 --> 00:06:31,312
We're about halfway down.

106
00:06:31,382 --> 00:06:34,272
So before we go deeper, take a guess.

107
00:06:34,342 --> 00:06:37,512
What do you think really sank the Edmund Fitzgerald?

108
00:06:37,582 --> 00:06:38,592
The storm?

109
00:06:38,662 --> 00:06:39,802
The hatch covers?

110
00:06:39,872 --> 00:06:41,382
A shoal?

111
00:06:41,452 --> 00:06:43,492
Or something wrong with the ship herself?

112
00:06:43,562 --> 00:06:46,412
Put your guess in the comments right now.

113
00:06:46,482 --> 00:06:48,092
One word is enough.

114
00:06:48,162 --> 00:06:51,692
Then, at the end, see if you've changed your mind.

115
00:06:51,762 --> 00:06:56,308
And stay with me, because below this, the wrecks get bigger,

116
00:06:56,328 --> 00:06:58,742
and the dark becomes the killer.

117
00:06:59,812 --> 00:07:05,872
As the water falls toward 200 feet, a steel freighter appears, the John B.

118
00:07:05,942 --> 00:07:06,272
Cowle.

119
00:07:06,342 --> 00:07:07,330
In July 1909,

120
00:07:07,350 --> 00:07:12,992
she collided with another freighter right here in Whitefish Bay and sank.

121
00:07:13,062 --> 00:07:15,962
14 of her 24 crew died.

122
00:07:16,032 --> 00:07:17,607
And then, at 200 feet,

123
00:07:17,627 --> 00:07:22,682
the wreck treasure hunters have talked about for a century and a half.

124
00:07:22,752 --> 00:07:24,402
The Comet.

125
00:07:24,472 --> 00:07:30,052
On a clear, starlit night in August 1875, she rounded the Whitefish Point light,

126
00:07:30,072 --> 00:07:31,452
heading for the Soo.

127
00:07:32,862 --> 00:07:36,462
In the dusk, her lookout saw a steamer's lights ahead.

128
00:07:36,532 --> 00:07:38,637
Her captain turned to avoid them,

129
00:07:38,657 --> 00:07:42,502
and found himself crossing right in front of the other ship.

130
00:07:42,572 --> 00:07:47,322
The Canadian steamer Manitoba struck her and cut deep into her side.

131
00:07:47,392 --> 00:07:52,092
A witness said her upper decks crumbled and sank in about a minute.

132
00:07:52,162 --> 00:07:54,582
10 men died.

133
00:07:54,652 --> 00:07:58,598
In her hold were 500 tons of pig iron, some copper,

134
00:07:58,618 --> 00:08:01,632
and 70 tons of silver ore from Montana.

135
00:08:02,752 --> 00:08:08,832
People tried to salvage it in 1876, and again in 1938.

136
00:08:08,902 --> 00:08:12,052
Both times, they couldn't even find the wreck.

137
00:08:12,122 --> 00:08:15,492
Divers finally found her in the 1970s.

138
00:08:15,562 --> 00:08:20,692
But what happened to the silver ore is still unknown.

139
00:08:20,762 --> 00:08:24,432
Below 200 feet, the bottom of the bay falls away.

140
00:08:24,502 --> 00:08:29,588
And at the deepest point of the preserve, about 270 feet down,

141
00:08:29,608 --> 00:08:31,482
lies the Superior City.

142
00:08:32,572 --> 00:08:39,042
When she was launched in 1898, she was the largest ship ever built on fresh water.

143
00:08:39,112 --> 00:08:43,782
The whole town of Lorain, Ohio, crowded the riverbank to watch.

144
00:08:43,852 --> 00:08:46,425
At 9:10 on the night of August 20, 1920,

145
00:08:46,445 --> 00:08:50,832
she was heading down into Whitefish Bay with 7,600 tons of iron ore.

146
00:08:55,512 --> 00:08:59,132
In the dark, another freighter, the Willis L.

147
00:08:59,202 --> 00:09:01,292
King, was coming the other way.

148
00:09:01,362 --> 00:09:03,152
The King hit her in the side.

149
00:09:03,222 --> 00:09:06,682
Cold lake water rushed in and reached her boilers.

150
00:09:06,752 --> 00:09:10,352
They exploded, and her stern was nearly blown off.

151
00:09:10,422 --> 00:09:15,062
Most of the crew had run to the lifeboats, which hung right above the boilers.

152
00:09:15,132 --> 00:09:17,992
29 people died.

153
00:09:18,062 --> 00:09:20,762
28 men and one woman.

154
00:09:20,832 --> 00:09:22,702
Only 4 survived.

155
00:09:22,772 --> 00:09:26,618
One of them was blown straight off the ship by the explosion,

156
00:09:26,638 --> 00:09:29,152
and was found floating on a hatch cover.

157
00:09:29,222 --> 00:09:33,012
It was the worst collision in the history of Lake Superior.

158
00:09:33,082 --> 00:09:35,232
The dark, and the cold.

159
00:09:35,302 --> 00:09:36,692
Remember that number…

160
00:09:36,762 --> 00:09:39,435
29.

161
00:09:39,505 --> 00:09:44,372
Now the water keeps falling, past the preserve, past the mouth of the bay,

162
00:09:44,392 --> 00:09:46,485
and out into open Lake Superior.

163
00:09:47,205 --> 00:09:53,059
17 miles from Whitefish Point, just across the border in Canadian waters,

164
00:09:53,079 --> 00:09:54,185
530 feet down…

165
00:09:54,705 --> 00:09:56,255
she appears.

166
00:09:56,325 --> 00:09:59,485
The Edmund Fitzgerald.

167
00:09:59,555 --> 00:10:05,675
729 feet long, the largest ship ever to sink on the Great Lakes.

168
00:10:05,745 --> 00:10:10,935
And on her last night, all three of this graveyard's killers were waiting for her.

169
00:10:11,005 --> 00:10:11,755
The storm.

170
00:10:11,825 --> 00:10:18,805
Winds from the northwest at nearly hurricane force, and waves up to 35 feet.

171
00:10:19,135 --> 00:10:20,105
The dark.

172
00:10:20,175 --> 00:10:22,325
Snow had closed in that afternoon.

173
00:10:22,395 --> 00:10:25,465
She had lost both of her radars.

174
00:10:25,535 --> 00:10:27,400
She was running for Whitefish Point almost blind,

175
00:10:27,420 --> 00:10:29,823
and when her captain asked about the radio beacon on the point,

176
00:10:29,843 --> 00:10:32,515
the one ships used to find their way in, he learned it wasn't working.

177
00:10:37,625 --> 00:10:38,985
And the cold.

178
00:10:39,055 --> 00:10:44,025
29 men went down with her, and not one of them was ever recovered.

179
00:10:44,095 --> 00:10:46,235
This time, the lake kept them all.

180
00:10:46,305 --> 00:10:51,565
At 7:10, her captain said, "We are holding our own."

181
00:10:51,635 --> 00:10:53,805
Minutes later, she was gone.

182
00:10:53,875 --> 00:10:55,365
29 men.

183
00:10:55,435 --> 00:11:02,335
The same number of lives the Superior City lost in these waters, 55 years earlier.

184
00:11:02,405 --> 00:11:07,235
If that just changed the way you see the Fitzgerald, do two things for me right now.

185
00:11:07,305 --> 00:11:11,225
Tap the like button, and if you haven't yet, subscribe.

186
00:11:11,295 --> 00:11:13,092
For a small channel like this one,

187
00:11:13,112 --> 00:11:18,275
that's the difference between this story reaching a few hundred people or a few hundred thousand.

188
00:11:20,147 --> 00:11:23,107
But there may have been a fourth killer that night.

189
00:11:23,177 --> 00:11:28,857
After our last Fitzgerald video, some of you who know these boats pointed me to it.

190
00:11:28,927 --> 00:11:31,017
The ship herself.

191
00:11:31,087 --> 00:11:32,176
When she was built,

192
00:11:32,196 --> 00:11:36,557
she was designed to ride with a certain amount of her hull above the water.

193
00:11:36,627 --> 00:11:38,587
That's called freeboard.

194
00:11:38,657 --> 00:11:41,498
But in 1969, 1971 and 1973, the Coast Guard raised her load line,

195
00:11:41,518 --> 00:11:45,637
allowing her to carry heavier loads and ride more than 3 feet deeper than her original design.

196
00:11:53,077 --> 00:11:58,141
On her last night, her deck was only about 11.5 feet above the water,

197
00:11:58,161 --> 00:12:00,057
in waves of up to 35 feet.

198
00:12:00,907 --> 00:12:05,647
Her steward, who sailed on her for 10 seasons and looked after her through 7 winters,

199
00:12:05,667 --> 00:12:07,887
later testified that her keel was loose.

200
00:12:10,027 --> 00:12:12,667
He said parts of it were only tack welded,

201
00:12:12,687 --> 00:12:15,707
and that he had seen many of those welds broken.

202
00:12:15,777 --> 00:12:19,227
He was never asked to testify at the official inquiry.

203
00:12:19,297 --> 00:12:22,665
And one of the engineers who helped design her hull later studied all of it,

204
00:12:22,685 --> 00:12:26,277
and concluded that on November 10, 1975, the Edmund Fitzgerald was not seaworthy.

205
00:12:33,467 --> 00:12:35,397
That's not the official answer.

206
00:12:35,467 --> 00:12:39,307
The Coast Guard blamed water coming in through her hatch covers.

207
00:12:39,377 --> 00:12:44,617
Others blame the waves alone, or a shoal she may have struck that day.

208
00:12:44,687 --> 00:12:46,847
And the wreck can't settle it.

209
00:12:46,917 --> 00:12:48,467
She lies in two pieces.

210
00:12:48,537 --> 00:12:51,577
Her bow stands upright in the mud.

211
00:12:51,647 --> 00:12:57,137
Her stern lies upside down, about 170 feet away.

212
00:12:57,207 --> 00:13:01,717
Between them is a field of iron ore pellets and torn steel.

213
00:13:01,787 --> 00:13:04,127
Her bell isn't down there anymore.

214
00:13:04,197 --> 00:13:10,007
In 1995, it was raised and taken to the museum at Whitefish Point.

215
00:13:10,077 --> 00:13:15,897
A replica was left in its place, engraved with the names of all 29 men.

216
00:13:15,967 --> 00:13:20,657
Under Ontario law, no one can dive on her without a license.

217
00:13:20,727 --> 00:13:23,875
She's a grave.

218
00:13:23,945 --> 00:13:26,405
Now let the water back in.

219
00:13:26,475 --> 00:13:30,055
Lake Superior rises over the Fitzgerald first.

220
00:13:30,125 --> 00:13:32,515
Then the deep wrecks of the preserve.

221
00:13:32,585 --> 00:13:34,295
The Superior City disappears.

222
00:13:34,365 --> 00:13:38,235
Then the Comet, and whatever is left of her silver.

223
00:13:38,305 --> 00:13:41,485
The Cowle, the Vienna, the Niagara.

224
00:13:41,555 --> 00:13:44,565
The Myron and the Miztec slip under together.

225
00:13:44,635 --> 00:13:46,906
Then the Saturn's broken timbers,

226
00:13:46,926 --> 00:13:50,515
and the water is back at the foot of the lighthouse.

227
00:13:50,585 --> 00:13:51,281
Tomorrow,

228
00:13:51,301 --> 00:13:57,565
freighters will pass Whitefish Point the way they have for more than 170 years.

229
00:13:57,865 --> 00:14:01,525
Every one of them sails over this graveyard.

230
00:14:01,595 --> 00:14:07,075
And once you know the story, you never look at that water the same way again.

231
00:14:07,145 --> 00:14:11,047
If you've sailed past Whitefish Point, worked on the boats, dived these wrecks,

232
00:14:11,067 --> 00:14:14,125
or visited the museum there, I'd really like to hear from you.

233
00:14:16,605 --> 00:14:20,675
Stories from people like you are the best part of this channel.

234
00:14:20,745 --> 00:14:23,425
Tell me yours in the comments.

235
00:14:23,495 --> 00:14:26,775
So, what sank the Edmund Fitzgerald?

236
00:14:26,845 --> 00:14:29,839
The storm, the dark and the cold were all there that night,

237
00:14:29,859 --> 00:14:33,825
the same three that have been sinking ships off Whitefish Point for 200 years.

238
00:14:35,975 --> 00:14:37,495
But was it the waves?

239
00:14:37,565 --> 00:14:38,575
The hatch covers?

240
00:14:38,645 --> 00:14:39,845
A shoal?

241
00:14:39,915 --> 00:14:43,585
Or a ship that was carrying more than she was built for?

242
00:14:43,655 --> 00:14:45,235
Now you've heard all of it.

243
00:14:45,305 --> 00:14:48,775
Tell me in the comments what you think, and why.

244
00:14:48,845 --> 00:14:52,105
And if you guessed earlier, tell me if you've changed your mind.

245
00:14:52,175 --> 00:14:54,115
I read every one.

246
00:14:54,185 --> 00:14:56,650
And if you've watched all the way to here,

